/**
 * Freehand marks.
 *
 * The intro is a DRAWING being drawn — `public/draw.mp4` is a hand-drawn
 * flame and `Mark.tsx` is traced from the real artwork. That hand then
 * disappeared: everything after the first 4.8 seconds was type, hairline
 * rules and space. These carry it into the page.
 *
 * ---- Why these are not an icon set ----
 * The obvious move is a licensed pack (the brief pointed at Streamline's
 * Freehand Duotone). Two reasons not to. Those sets are paid-tier, and
 * more importantly DUOTONE spends the flame in a dozen new places — and
 * the palette rule is that the accent is the one saturated colour on the
 * page, so the more places it appears the less any of them mean. These
 * are strokes only, in ash, and the heat warms them like every other
 * rule on the page.
 *
 * ---- Why the path data looks like that ----
 * Every mark was drafted as clean geometry and then put through a
 * roughening pass that displaces it along its own normal with smooth
 * noise. DO NOT HAND-EDIT THE OUTPUT — redraft the clean shape and
 * re-run the pass, exactly as with Mark.tsx's trace.
 *
 * The pass is `tools/marks/rough.mjs`, and the clean geometry for the
 * ten newest decorative marks is `tools/marks/hero-marks.mjs` — run it
 * and paste. Everything above `sparkle` predates that file and would
 * need its shape written there first.
 *
 * Three things that pass got wrong first, all of them visible:
 *   - A flat amplitude everywhere turned every mark into a POTATO. A
 *     drawn line is confident: proportions stay accurate, corners stay
 *     corners, and only the runs between them wander.
 *   - A corner is a SPIKE in turn rate, not a large angle. Judged
 *     against a fixed threshold, every sample on a small circle read as
 *     a corner and the circle came out a heptagon.
 *   - Amplitude has to scale with the feature. A fixed wobble is a
 *     quarter of a 2-unit pupil's radius and invisible on a 60-unit
 *     outline.
 *
 * ---- How they are driven ----
 * The wrapper carries `data-heat="draw"`, and the field writes `color`
 * and `--draw-w` on it every frame. Strokes are `currentColor` at
 * `var(--draw-w)`, so one write drives colour AND weight — the same
 * two-channel idea as the type's weight and width axes, which is the
 * argument the rest of the page already makes.
 *
 * The wrapper is also load-bearing for ScrollReveal: an `<svg>` holds no
 * text and contains no descendant `img`/`svg`, so it can never be a
 * content block on its own. Without the div these would be the only
 * things on the page that do not come up into it.
 */

